## Lawvere's fixed-point theorem (1969)

F. William Lawvere, "Diagonal arguments and cartesian closed categories" (Lecture Notes in Mathematics 92,
1969). In a cartesian closed category: if there is a map φ : A → B^A that is point-surjective (every map
A → B is φ(a) for some point a), then every map f : B → B has a fixed point. The proof is one line: take
g(a) = f(φ(a)(a)); g = φ(a₀) for some a₀; then g(a₀) = f(g(a₀)).

Read contrapositively: if B has a map without a fixed point, no A can reach all of B^A. Each classical
limitative result is an instance:

- Cantor (1891): B = 2, f = negation; no set surjects onto its power set.
- Russell (1901): the set of all sets that are not members of themselves.
- Gödel (1931) and Tarski (1933): B = sentences or truth values, f = negation; a sufficiently expressive
  language cannot define its own truth.
- Turing (1936): B = halts / does not halt, f = the machine that does the opposite; no program decides
  halting for all programs.

What all of them say: a system that can name its own naming meets a fixed point it cannot escape by
negation.

## Bhartṛhari (5th century)

Grammarian and philosopher of language in Pāṇini's tradition; his work is the Vākyapadīya, "Treatise on
Sentences and Words". His positions:

- No cognition comes without the word: knowing is shot through with language, not labelled by it
  afterwards.
- The sentence is the unit of meaning; words are abstractions cut out of sentences; meaning is grasped
  whole, in one flash, while the sounds that reveal it arrive in order.
- The Word is the ground of reality: distinctions among things are distinctions of language.
- The paradox. Some schools (the Naiyāyikas: "whatever is, is knowable and nameable") held that
  everything can be named; Buddhist philosophers held that some things cannot. Bhartṛhari saw that the
  unnameability thesis cannot be verified by an instance, since giving the instance names what it calls
  unnameable. He applies it to two relations: the relation of signification cannot itself be signified,
  and the relation of inherence cannot be signified. The same text treats the liar paradox family.
- The exit. He identifies a hidden parameter whose neglect produces the paradox and whose restoration
  dissolves it: the act of signifying and its extension in time. The paradox (contradiction, or infinite
  regress) arises when that act is abstracted from its moment by assuming a simultaneous, opposite
  function. Signifying is an act at a moment; the act that names "the unnameable" is not the act it
  names.

## The literature

- Hans G. Herzberger and Radhika Herzberger, "Bhartrhari's Paradox", Journal of Indian Philosophy 9:1
  (March 1981), 1–17; reprinted in Studies in Indian Philosophy (L. D. Institute of Indology, Ahmedabad).
  They develop his remarks into a paradox in the modern sense and connect it directly to Russell's paradox
  and to the axiom of regularity, by which axiomatic set theory forbids sets that are members of
  themselves.
- Jan E. M. Houben, "Paradox and perspectivism in the philosophy of language of Bhartṛhari": his hidden
  parameter suits Barwise and Etchemendy's solution to the liar (The Liar, 1989), where a proposition is
  evaluated in a situation.
- A later reply (PhilPapers record PARBOW) argues that the appeal to set theory does not by itself settle
  his claim about signification, and that arguments akin to the semantic paradoxes, such as the liar, come
  much closer.

## The correspondence (Claude's construction, at Avik's request)

- "The relation of signification cannot be signified" is Tarski's undefinability in the theory of
  language: a language cannot contain its own semantics. In Lawvere's terms, words A, the properties of
  words B^A, negation the fixed-point-free map on B; calling a thing unnameable forces the fixed point,
  because the call names it.
- The two exits differ. Set theory forbids the self-reference (regularity). Bhartṛhari keeps it and indexes
  it to its occurrence: meaning is relative to the act and its moment. That is the receive relation of
  Part I (meaning depends on the receiver's state), and it is homotopy type theory's way, which keeps
  self-application and makes truth a construction in context, not set theory's prohibition.
- Fourteen centuries lie between Bhartṛhari and Russell (indian-knowledge-history).
